      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Since antileukotriene treatment for asthma was introduced, there has been debate about whether such therapy can lead to Churg-Strauss Syndrome (CSS) or whether CSS is simply inhibited by the use of steroids, as various authors have suggested. We report a case in which we suspected CSS in a patient with bronchopulmonary, cutaneous and analytical signs and whom we treated with oral steroids. After clinical improvement, one year later, steroids were replaced by antileukotrienes, after which the same clinical picture developed. The vasculitis characteristic of CSS was confirmed pathologically."},
